
# Works for: Student / Employee / Product Management Systems
# Stack: HTML + CSS + Vanilla JS + Node.js + Express + DynamoDB








Create DynamoDB Table
1. Go to: https://console.aws.amazon.com/dynamodb
2. Click "Create table"
3. Table name:        Students        ← Change this per exam question
4. Partition key:     StudentID       ← Type: String
5. Leave all other settings as default (On-demand capacity)
6. Click "Create table"
7. Wait ~30 seconds for table to become Active


Create IAM User for Credentials (Local Development)
1. Go to: https://console.aws.amazon.com/iam
2. Click Users → Create user
3. Username: crud-app-user (any name)
4. Click Next
5. Select "Attach policies directly"
6. Search and attach: AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess
7. Click Create user
8. Click on the user → Security credentials tab
9. Click "Create access key"
10. Choose "Local code" → Create
11. SAVE the Access Key ID and Secret Access Key (you need these below)




 Install Node.js (if not installed)
Download from: https://nodejs.org  (LTS version)
Verify: node --version

### Step 2: Set up project folder
mkdir student-crud-app
cd student-crud-app
mkdir public

Then copy:
  server.js       → student-crud-app/server.js
  package.json    → student-crud-app/package.json
  index.html      → student-crud-app/public/index.html

### Step 3: Install dependencies
cd student-crud-app
npm install

### Step 4: Configure AWS Credentials (Option A - recommended for local)
Run this command and enter your keys when prompted:
  aws configure

  AWS Access Key ID:     [paste your key]
  AWS Secret Access Key: [paste your secret]
  Default region name:   us-east-1
  Default output format: json

If AWS CLI not installed:
  https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html

### Step 5: Run the server
node server.js

### Step 6: Open in browser
http://localhost:3000


## PART 3: DEPLOY ON AWS EC2
==============================

### Step 1: Launch EC2 Instance
1. Go to: https://console.aws.amazon.com/ec2
2. Click "Launch instance"
3. Name: student-crud-server
4. AMI: Amazon Linux 2023 (Free Tier eligible)
5. Instance type: t2.micro (Free Tier)
6. Key pair: Create new → Download .pem file (SAVE IT!)
7. Network settings → "Allow HTTP traffic" ✓
8. Also add custom rule: Port 3000, Source: Anywhere (0.0.0.0/0)
   (Click "Edit" in Security Groups → Add rule → Custom TCP → Port 3000)
9. Click "Launch instance"

### Step 2: Attach IAM Role to EC2 (so EC2 can access DynamoDB)
1. Go to IAM → Roles → Create role
2. Trusted entity: AWS service → EC2
3. Attach policy: AmazonDynamoDBFullAccess
4. Role name: EC2DynamoDBRole → Create role
5. Go to EC2 → Select your instance
6. Actions → Security → Modify IAM role
7. Select EC2DynamoDBRole → Update

(With an IAM Role, you do NOT need to run "aws configure" on EC2)

### Step 3: Connect to EC2 via SSH
# On Mac/Linux:
chmod 400 your-key.pem
ssh -i "your-key.pem" ec2-user@<EC2-PUBLIC-IP>

# On Windows: Use PuTTY or VS Code Remote SSH or Windows Terminal

### Step 4: Install Node.js on EC2
# Run these commands on EC2:
curl -fsSL https://rpm.nodesource.com/setup_18.x | sudo bash -
sudo yum install -y nodejs
node --version    # Verify

### Step 5: Upload your project to EC2
# Option A: From your LOCAL machine (run this locally, not on EC2):
scp -i "your-key.pem" -r ./student-crud-app ec2-user@<EC2-PUBLIC-IP>:~/

# Option B: Git (if project is on GitHub):
# On EC2: git clone https://github.com/yourusername/student-crud-app.git

### Step 6: Run the app on EC2
cd student-crud-app
npm install
node server.js

### Step 7: Access the app
Open browser → http://<EC2-PUBLIC-IP>:3000

Find your EC2 Public IP:
  EC2 Console → Instances → Click your instance → "Public IPv4 address"

### Step 8: Keep server running after SSH disconnect (optional for exam)
# Install pm2:
sudo npm install -g pm2

# Start with pm2:
pm2 start server.js
pm2 save

# Now app runs even after you close SSH terminal


## PART 4: HOW TO CHANGE TO EMPLOYEE SYSTEM
============================================

### In server.js — change only 1 line:
  FROM: const TABLE_NAME = "Students";
  TO:   const TABLE_NAME = "Employees";

  Also change the route paths (optional for exam):
  FROM: /api/students
  TO:   /api/employees

  And the partition key in PUT/DELETE:
  FROM: Key: { StudentID: req.params.id }
  TO:   Key: { EmployeeID: req.params.id }

  And in POST:
  FROM: StudentID: uuidv4()
  TO:   EmployeeID: uuidv4()

### In public/index.html — change these:
  - <title>Student Management System</title>  → Employee...
  - <h1>🎓 Student Management System</h1>   → 👔 Employee...
  - Placeholder text in inputs
  - Column headers in <thead>
  - In JS: references to s.StudentID → s.EmployeeID

### In DynamoDB:
  - Create a new table called "Employees"
  - Partition key: EmployeeID (String)


## QUICK REFERENCE: COMMON EXAM MISTAKES TO AVOID
===================================================
1. Security Group must allow port 3000 (not just 80)
2. DynamoDB table region must match server.js region
3. IAM Role must be attached to EC2 (or aws configure run manually)
4. Run "npm install" before "node server.js"
5. Use EC2 Public IP, not Private IP, in browser URL
6. If port 3000 is blocked: change PORT in server.js to 80 and use http://<IP>
